**Goal** — Add the injectable Actions-job helpers used by the CI wait to identify and re-run runner-starved jobs.

**Motivation** — Re-running is usually enough.

**Approach** — Add `packages/nax/src/workflow/ci-starved.ts` with `CiRun`, `ActionsJob`, `actionsJobOf`, `isStarvedJob`, `rerunJob` and `CI_STARVED_RERUN_LIMIT` (2). Each helper takes the `run` it calls, so the CI wait can pass `_ciWaitDeps.run`.

For each check in the `cancel` bucket whose `link` is an Actions job URL it runs `gh api --hostname <host> repos/<owner>/<repo>/actions/jobs/<jobId>` and calls the job **starved** when `conclusion` is `cancelled`, `runner_name` is empty or null, and `steps` is an empty array. A check in the `fail` bucket is never starved. A check whose job cannot be read is not starved (fail closed: it stays a failure).

The rerun API is `gh api --hostname <host> -X POST repos/<owner>/<repo>/actions/jobs/<jobId>/rerun`. A cancelled check has no `link`, or its `link` is not an Actions job URL: Not starved; the check stays a failure. The job read exits non-zero, prints unparseable JSON, or the reply has no `steps` array: Not starved; the check stays a failure. Mirror readJobSteps's fail-soft command/JSON handling, using the supplied runner for every external call. No existing source file needs modification in this story.

**Scope** — In: URL recognition, fail-closed job reads, boolean POST result, limit constant and helper tests. Out: poll classification, ledger, deadline and logs (US-002); readiness consumers (US-003/004/007/008); waiver records (US-005). GitLab: a cancelled GitLab job or pipeline is never classed as starved and is never re-run. Re-running a starved job outside a CI wait, as a command of its own. A config key for the re-run limit: `CI_STARVED_RERUN_LIMIT` is a constant, as `CI_ERROR_POLL_LIMIT` is.

**Interface** —
```ts
export const CI_STARVED_RERUN_LIMIT = 2;
/** The forge runner, declared here so this module needs no import from ci-wait.ts. */
export type CiRun = (cmd: string[], opts: { cwd: string }) => Promise<ForgeRunResult>;
export interface ActionsJob { readonly host: string; readonly repo: string; readonly jobId: string }
/** `undefined` for any link that is not `https://<host>/<owner>/<repo>/actions/runs/<n>/job/<n>`. */
export function actionsJobOf(link: string): ActionsJob | undefined;
/** True only for a cancelled job with no runner and no steps; any read failure is false. */
export function isStarvedJob(job: ActionsJob, cwd: string, run: CiRun): Promise<boolean>;
/** True when the re-run request exited 0. */
export function rerunJob(job: ActionsJob, cwd: string, run: CiRun): Promise<boolean>;
```

**Goal** — Make waitForChecks distinguish GitHub runner starvation from failure, issue at most two opt-in rerun rounds, and return a head-bound starved verdict when recovery cannot finish.

**Motivation** — A starved runner is reported as a CI failure.

**Approach** — The classification lives in the CI wait, so every caller of `waitForChecks` sees the same outcome. The GitHub checks read asks for one more field, `link`, which for an Actions check is the job URL `https://<host>/<owner>/<repo>/actions/runs/<runId>/job/<jobId>`; each parsed GitHub check carries its `state` and `link`. Only when a poll would end the wait as `failed` (a failed check and nothing pending) does the wait look at jobs. For each check in the `cancel` bucket whose `link` is an Actions job URL it runs `gh api --hostname <host> repos/<owner>/<repo>/actions/jobs/<jobId>` and calls the job **starved** when `conclusion` is `cancelled`, `runner_name` is empty or null, and `steps` is an empty array. A check in the `fail` bucket is never starved. A check whose job cannot be read is not starved (fail closed: it stays a failure).

The wait keeps, for its own lifetime, the number of re-run rounds it has issued and the set of job ids it has re-run. On a would-be `failed` poll:

1. A cancelled check whose job id is one this wait already re-ran is a re-run GitHub has not reported yet. It gets no job read and is set aside; if any such check is set aside and every other failed check is starved, the poll is not terminal, the wait keeps polling, and no round is spent.
2. Otherwise, if any failed check is not starved, the wait ends `failed`, exactly as today.
3. If every failed check is starved, the caller passed `rerunStarved: true`, and fewer than `CI_STARVED_RERUN_LIMIT` rounds have run, the wait re-runs every starved job (`gh api --hostname <host> -X POST repos/<owner>/<repo>/actions/jobs/<jobId>/rerun`), records the job ids, logs one info line, and keeps polling. A re-run request that fails ends the wait `starved` at once.
4. Otherwise the wait ends `starved`, with `starvedChecks` naming the checks.

A starved verdict is bound to the head like a green one. When `expectedSha` is given, a would-be `failed` poll on GitHub also reads the PR head (`gh pr view <prRef> --json headRefOid`, as `githubWaiting` already does for green and waiting polls); when the head it reports differs from `expectedSha`, the wait does not classify or re-run anything and ends `unavailable` with `checks report <sha8> but HEAD is <sha8>`. A head that cannot be read leaves the poll as it is (fail open, as for green).

At the deadline, when at least one round ran and every non-green check of the latest poll is a check this wait re-ran (matched by name) that is either still in the `pending` bucket with a `state` other than `IN_PROGRESS`, or still reports a job id this wait re-ran, the wait ends `starved` instead of `pending`. Any other deadline is unchanged.

All of this lives in `packages/nax/src/workflow/ci-starved.ts`, behind two helpers that `waitForChecks` calls at exactly one site each: `decideStarved` where a GitHub poll would end the wait `failed`, and `starvedAtDeadline` where the deadline is reached. The wait's bookkeeping is an immutable `StarvedLedger` value that each `decideStarved` call returns anew. The GitHub `failed` and `waiting` polls carry the parsed checks (`ghChecks`) so both helpers can read `name`, `bucket`, `state` and `link`. `ci-wait.ts` is 513 lines against the 600-line limit, and `waitForChecks` must not gain branches that fail `check:complexity`. The new tests for the wait go in a new file, `packages/nax/test/unit/workflow/ci-wait-starved.test.ts`, because `ci-wait.test.ts` is 765 lines against the 800-line test limit.

The `starved` detail is the tail only; each surface adds its own `CI starved:` / `ci starved:` prefix, as it already does for `failed`. With no round run it is `<n> check(s) never got a runner: <a>; <b>; re-run them, or accept with --waive-starved <reason>`, and after `k` rounds it is `<n> check(s) never got a runner: <a>; <b> (re-run k time(s)); accept with --waive-starved <reason>`. When a re-run request fails it is `<n> check(s) never got a runner: <a>; <b> (the re-run request failed); accept with --waive-starved <reason>`. Check names are joined with `; ` because a check name may itself contain a comma.

A re-run request that fails emits a `warn` line `CI wait: could not re-run <name>` with `storyId: "_run"` first, before returning starved. The successful round's info line is `CI wait: re-running <n> check(s) that never got a runner: <a>; <b> (round <k> of 2)` with the same run scope. Job reads and re-runs go through `_ciWaitDeps.run` too. `starvedChecks` is present exactly when `outcome` is `"starved"`. New optional fields are spread in only when set, so payloads without them are exactly today's. Update the two existing checks argv assertions at ci-wait.test.ts lines 65 and 326 only to append link; every other assertion keeps passing and no new tests are added there. Ensure the end-result wrapper preserves starvedChecks, and keep heartbeat cleanup and abort behavior.

**Scope** — In: wait result/option/check extensions; helper-based decisions, immutable ledger, deadline, head binding and logs; new wait tests. Out: ciAllowsReady waiver policy (US-004), caller opt-in and publish reasons (US-003/007), durable waiver records (US-005), status/promote/bot CLI (US-006/007/008). GitLab: a cancelled GitLab job or pipeline is never classed as starved and is never re-run. `readChecksOnce` (the one-shot read behind `nax bot fix`'s gate baseline) keeps its current outcomes; a starved check there still reads as `failed`. A check still queued at the deadline that this wait never re-ran stays `pending`, as today. A failed check seen while other checks are still pending, at the deadline or at the error-poll limit, keeps today's `failed` outcome without a job read. A config key for the re-run limit: `CI_STARVED_RERUN_LIMIT` is a constant, as `CI_ERROR_POLL_LIMIT` is.

**Interface** —
```ts
export type CiWaitOutcome = "green" | "failed" | "pending" | "conflicting" | "no-checks" | "unavailable" | "starved";
export interface CiWaitResult { readonly outcome: CiWaitOutcome; readonly detail: string; readonly starvedChecks?: readonly string[] }
// waitForChecks options: the existing cwd, timeoutMs, pollMs, signal?, expectedSha? plus
// readonly rerunStarved?: boolean (absent means false).
interface GhCheck { readonly name: string; readonly bucket: string; readonly state: string; readonly link?: string }
export interface StarvedCheckView { readonly name: string; readonly bucket: string; readonly state: string; readonly link?: string }
export interface StarvedLedger { readonly rounds: number; readonly rerunJobIds: ReadonlySet<string>; readonly rerunNames: ReadonlySet<string> }
export const EMPTY_STARVED_LEDGER: StarvedLedger;
export type StarvedDecision =
  | { readonly kind: "failed" }
  | { readonly kind: "continue"; readonly ledger: StarvedLedger }
  | { readonly kind: "starved"; readonly detail: string; readonly starvedChecks: readonly string[] };
export function decideStarved(input: {
  readonly checks: readonly StarvedCheckView[];
  readonly ledger: StarvedLedger;
  readonly rerun: boolean;
  readonly cwd: string;
  readonly run: CiRun;
}): Promise<StarvedDecision>;
/** The `starved` result for the deadline rule, or undefined when the deadline is unchanged. */
export function starvedAtDeadline(checks: readonly StarvedCheckView[], ledger: StarvedLedger): CiWaitResult | undefined;
```

**Goal** — Add a strict, own-comment CI waiver record and reusable reason validation.

**Motivation** — The only escape was `gh pr ready` by hand, which leaves `nax workflow status` at `outcome: draft` and no record of why a merged PR carries a check that never ran.

**Approach** — A caller that readies a PR under a waiver first posts a `w-ci-waiver` comment (`renderCiWaiver`) and marks the PR ready only when that post succeeded. `nax workflow status` reads the newest own `w-ci-waiver` comment that parses (`latestCiWaiver`) on the live read and reports it. The record mirrors the `w-coverage` record (`packages/nax/src/bot/coverage-record.ts`): a heading with a declared count, one code-span line per check, and here one `Reason:` line; a body whose parts disagree reads as no record. This story supplies the record helpers; callers and posting are other stories.

Add `packages/nax/src/workflow/ci-waiver.ts` (`CI_WAIVER_KEY`, `CI_WAIVER_REASON_MAX`, `parseWaiverReason`, `renderCiWaiver`, `parseCiWaiver`, `latestCiWaiver`) and add `w-ci-waiver` to `TOP_LEVEL_MARKER_KEYS`.

### File Format — the `w-ci-waiver` comment

`renderCiWaiver({ headSha: <40-hex sha>, checks: ["nax-agent: glob (Node 22.19.0, macos-latest)"], reason: "runner starvation, re-run passed" })` returns `withNaxBotMarker(record, "w-ci-waiver")` where `record` is:

```
CI waiver at 7c98673abc0123456789abcdef0123456789abcd: 1 check(s) never got a runner
- `nax-agent: glob (Node 22.19.0, macos-latest)`
Reason: runner starvation, re-run passed
```

- The heading is `CI waiver at <40-hex sha>: <n> check(s) never got a runner`.
- One code-span check line per check (`- ` then the name in backticks); a backtick inside a name is written as `'`, and an empty name is written as `(unnamed check)`.
- The last line is `Reason: ` followed by `neutralise(reason)`.

`parseCiWaiver` strips the markers, finds the heading, reads the check lines under it, then the `Reason: ` line. It returns `null` when there is no heading, when the number of check lines differs from the declared count, when the `Reason: ` line is missing, or when any non-blank line follows it.

The newest own `w-ci-waiver` comment does not parse: `latestCiWaiver` skips it and returns the next newest that parses.

`--waive-starved` with an empty, multi-line or over-300-character reason, or one holding `<!--`: `NaxError` `CI_WAIVER_REASON_INVALID`; exit 1 before any forge call. The helper validates and throws; CLI exit behavior belongs to US-007/008. Respect 300-character round-trip reasons: do not use capped inline rendering or revalidate an already neutralised record as raw input. For the record-line assertion, remove the standard withNaxBotMarker wrapper (its visible **nax bot** header, surrounding blank lines and trailing identity marker) before comparing the heading, check and Reason lines. stripNaxBotMarkers alone leaves the visible header; do not change that shared helper or omit the required wrapper to make this comparison pass. Append `"w-ci-waiver"` as the sixth, last entry in `TOP_LEVEL_MARKER_KEYS`, updating the exact pinned list at threads-comments.test.ts line 72.

**Scope** — In: reason parser, marker-key recognition, record renderer/parser, newest valid own-comment selection and helper tests. Out: CI polling (US-001/002), ready predicate (US-004), command validation/writes (US-007/008), status reporting (US-006). `latestCiWaiver` reads only comments by the current forge login, as the item index does; a waiver posted by another account is not shown. A check name containing a backtick does not round-trip through the waiver record: the backtick is written as `'`.

**Interface** —
```ts
export const CI_WAIVER_KEY = "w-ci-waiver";
export const CI_WAIVER_REASON_MAX = 300;
export interface CiWaiver { readonly headSha: string; readonly checks: readonly string[]; readonly reason: string }
/** The trimmed reason; throws NaxError CI_WAIVER_REASON_INVALID for an empty, multi-line, over-long or `<!--`-holding one. */
export function parseWaiverReason(raw: string): string;
/** The marked comment body: the record, wrapped by withNaxBotMarker(…, CI_WAIVER_KEY). */
export function renderCiWaiver(waiver: CiWaiver): string;
export function parseCiWaiver(body: string): CiWaiver | null;
/** The newest comment by `login` with marker key `w-ci-waiver` that parses; null when none does. */
export function latestCiWaiver(comments: readonly PrComment[], login: string): CiWaiver | null;
```

**Goal** — Add a validated starved-only workflow promotion waiver with visible dry-run checks and a durable comment action before ready.

**Motivation** — Nothing can release the PR.

**Approach** — Add the CLI option; validate it before the run is loaded; `PromoteTarget` keeps the CI result; the `ci-green` check passes a `starved` CI when the reason is given; a passing check prints its detail when it has one; `--yes` adds the `waiver` action between `comment` and `ready`.

`nax workflow promote -f <feature> [--yes] --waive-starved <reason>`: the reason is validated before the run is loaded; an invalid reason prints the error and exits 1. The `ci-green` check passes for a `starved` CI with `detail` `waived: <a>; <b>` and prints `pass  ci-green  waived: <a>; <b>`. With `--yes` the actions are `tick`, `comment`, `waiver`, `ready`, `record`; `waiver` posts the waiver comment and is `skipped` when CI was green. Without the flag there is no `waiver` action and a starved CI fails the check: `FAIL  ci-green  ci starved: <detail>`.

The re-run is opt-in per caller. The run's publish (`waitForCi` in `packages/nax/src/workflow/finalize.ts`) and `nax bot fix` (`waitForCi` in `packages/nax/src/bot/fix-report.ts`) pass `rerunStarved: true`. `nax workflow promote` does not: it is a one-poll check and, without `--yes`, a report that must not write to the forge.

`WorkflowPromoteOptions` (`packages/nax/src/cli/workflow-promote.ts`): target adds `readonly waiveStarved?: string`. `PromoteTarget` (`packages/nax/src/cli/workflow-promote.ts`): target adds `readonly ci: CiWaitResult` (the wait `ciGreen` read) and `readonly headSha: string` (the `expectedSha` that wait was bound to), so the `waiver` action can name the checks and the head.

`PromoteAction["name"]` and `ForgeAction` (`packages/nax/src/cli/workflow-promote.ts`): target each adds `"waiver"`; the action order is `tick`, `comment`, `waiver`, `ready`, `record`, and `waiver` appears only when `--waive-starved` was given.

`checkLine` (`packages/nax/src/cli/workflow-promote.ts`) — Target: a passing check prints `pass  <name>` when its detail is empty and `pass  <name>  <detail>` otherwise; a failing check is unchanged.

A caller that readies a PR under a waiver first posts a `w-ci-waiver` comment (`renderCiWaiver`) and marks the PR ready only when that post succeeded. Render from the retained CI result's starvedChecks and retained headSha, use `commentArgv` for the target PR, and execute through `forgeWrite` so failures stop the ordered action list. Posting the waiver comment fails in `nax workflow promote --yes`: The promotion stops at `waiver` (`promote stopped at waiver: <stderr>`); `ready` and `record` never run. When CI is green the flag changes nothing except a skipped waiver action in a real promotion. Actual failures are never waived.

Validate with `parseWaiverReason` before resolveOutputDir/loadRunVerdict/waitForChecks; invalid empty, multiline, over-300-character or <!---holding reasons surface CI_WAIVER_REASON_INVALID and return 1 before forge calls. Thread the optional option through the promote command registration in cli/workflow.ts and conditionally spread new optional fields. Without `--waive-starved` the promote checks, plain lines and actions are today's (no `waiver` action); the existing promote tests pin that path. Use workflowPromoteCommand/registered CLI with `forgeDeps.waitForChecks` stubbed, and assert parsed comment content, dry-run no-write behavior and action error ordering.

**Scope** — In: promote CLI option/early reason validation, retained head/CI, waived check detail, conditional waiver action and failure stop. Out: wait classification (US-002), predicate/record helpers (US-004/005), bot fix (US-007), status (US-006). `nax workflow promote` never re-runs a job; only the run's publish and `nax bot fix` do.

**Interface** —
```ts
// WorkflowPromoteOptions adds:
readonly waiveStarved?: string;
// PromoteTarget adds:
readonly ci: CiWaitResult;
readonly headSha: string;
// PromoteAction["name"] target:
"tick" | "comment" | "waiver" | "ready" | "record"
// ForgeAction target:
"tick" | "comment" | "waiver" | "ready"
```
